<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Srirangapatna</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Seringapatam</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Srirangapatna, historically Seringapatam, is a town on an island in the Kaveri river near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mysore">Mysore</a> in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. It was the capital of the Wodeyar kings of Mysore from 1610 and then of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyder-ali">Hyder Ali</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tipu-sultan">Tipu Sultan</a>, who fortified it. British forces besieged it in 1792 and stormed it in 1799, when Tipu died defending the fort.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/srirangapatna.jpg" alt="Srirangapatna" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ms Sarah Welch · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gopuram_of_the_Ranganathaswamy_Temple,_Srirangapatna_Karnataka_India.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Srirangapatna, also spelled Srirangapattana, is a town in Mandya district in the Indian state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a>, on an island in the Kaveri river, near the city of Mandya. It takes its name from the Ranganathaswamy Temple, consecrated around 984 CE. Under British rule it was called Seringapatam. Karighatta, the Black Hill, rises a few kilometres outside the island town, reached by 450 stone steps to a hilltop temple of Vaikunta Srinivasa. The Nimishamba temple, to Parvathi, stands on the bank of the Lokapavani river.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Srirangapatna became the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mysore">Mysore</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyder-ali">Hyder Ali</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tipu-sultan">Tipu Sultan</a>. Tipu put aside his show of deference to the Wodeyar Maharaja, whom he in effect held captive, and proclaimed the Khudadad State under his own kingship, though Chamaraja Wodeyar IX was still officially king of Mysore. In his wars against the British, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kodava-people">Kodavas</a> and the Malabar rajas, he rounded up whole communities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nair">Nairs</a>, Kodavas and Mangalorean Catholics and deported them to Srirangapatna, where they were held in bondage until the British defeat of Tipu in 1799 freed them.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-seringapatam">Treaty of Seringapatam</a>, signed on 18 March 1792, ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-anglo-mysore-war">Third Anglo-Mysore War</a>. Its signatories included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-cornwallis-1st-marquess-cornwallis">Lord Cornwallis</a> for the British East India Company, representatives of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizam-of-hyderabad">Nizam of Hyderabad</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha Empire</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tipu-sultan">Tipu Sultan</a>.</p><p>In 1799 the town was the scene of the last and decisive battle of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fourth-anglo-mysore-war">Fourth Anglo-Mysore War</a>, fought between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tipu-sultan">Tipu Sultan</a> and a combined force of 50,000 men provided equally by the Nizam and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-harris-1st-baron-harris">General George Harris</a>. At the climax Tipu was killed within the fort, betrayed by one of his confidants. The victors plundered the town and ransacked Tipu's palace. In 1809 Lord Harris's House was the scene of a mutiny by Madras Army officers led by Col. Bell against the Governor of Madras, Sir George Barlow.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The fortress in the west of the island is surrounded by double walls, and an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/obelisk">obelisk</a> marks where the British broke through; the spot where Tipu fell has a memorial. The ramparts, the Water Gate and the site of the destroyed palace survive, as does Colonel Bailey's Dungeon, where British officers were held. The Garrison Cemetery holds about 307 graves of officers killed in the assault of 1799 and their families. The monuments of the island have been nominated as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> and the application is on the tentative list. The town appears in Wilkie Collins's The Moonstone.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Srirangapatna” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Tipu's Tiger, a wooden automaton of a tiger attacking a European soldier that contains a small organ, was taken from <b>Srirangapatna</b> and is now in the V&amp;A in London.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-mysore-1399-1799">Kingdom of Mysore (1399-1799) · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>The great nayakas of Madurai, Thanjavur and Gingee, and the Wodeyars of Mysore, who took <b>Srirangapatna</b> in 1610, now ruled largely as independent princes, giving only nominal allegiance.</blockquote><a href="/empire/vijayanagara-empire">Vijayanagara Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>After Tipu Sultan was killed at <b>Seringapatam</b> in 1799, the British restored the Hindu Wodeyar dynasty, which Hyder Ali had sidelined, and placed the five-year-old Krishnaraja Wodeyar III on the throne under the diwan Purnaiah.</blockquote><a href="/state/mysore-state">Mysore State</a></li></ul></section>
